    {
      "message": 2,
      "state": "state_1",
      "statesRecorded": 2,
      "text": "No! 🫀 The four heart valves do NOT open at the same time. They open and close in a coordinated sequence as your heart beats.\n\nThe valves work in two pairs.\n\n1. When the ventricles relax (diastole)\nOPEN\n\nTricuspid and mitral valves\n\nCLOSED\n\nPulmonary and aortic valves\n\nBlood flows from the atria into the ventricles.\n\n2. When the ventricles contract (systole)\nOPEN\n\nPulmonary and aortic valves\n\nCLOSED\n\nTricuspid and mitral valves\n\nBlood is pumped from the ventricles into the lungs and the rest of the body.\n\nSee how the valves work\nHeart relaxes\nHeart contracts\n\nThe ventricles eject blood through the pulmonary and aortic valves.\n\nEducational schematic, not an anatomical drawing. There are also brief moments when all four valves are closed.\nWhy don't all four open together?\n\nBecause blood needs to flow in one direction. If the inlet and outlet valves opened together at the wrong time, the heart couldn't build pressure and pump efficiently.\n\nOne more interesting fact: the valves don't open because the heart directly pulls them open. They open and close because of pressure differences on either side of each valve.\n\nQuick question: When the left ventricle contracts to pump blood into the ",
      "controls": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "tag": "button",
          "type": "button",
          "label": "Heart relaxes",
          "context": "Heart relaxes\nHeart contracts",
          "disabled": false,
          "checked": false,
          "explored": false
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "tag": "button",
          "type": "button",
          "label": "Heart contracts",
          "context": "Heart relaxes\nHeart contracts",
          "disabled": false,
          "checked": true,
          "explored": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "message": 3,
      "state": "state_0",
      "statesRecorded": 1,
      "text": "The heart does not purify or clean the blood directly. 🫀 Its main job is to pump blood to organs that perform different functions.\n\nThree important organs are involved:\n\n🫀 Heart: Pumps blood throughout the body.\n\n🫁 Lungs: Remove carbon dioxide from the blood and add oxygen.\n\nKidneys: Filter waste products and excess water from the blood, producing urine.\n\nThe liver also helps process toxins, medications, and waste substances.\n\nHow does it work?\n\nBody → Heart\n\nBlood returns with less oxygen and more carbon dioxide\n\nLungs\n\nCarbon dioxide is removed and oxygen is added\n\nHeart → Body\n\nOxygen-rich blood is pumped throughout the body\n\nMeanwhile, the kidneys continuously filter blood, removing waste and helping maintain the right balance of water and salts.\n\nRemember: The heart is the pump, the lungs are responsible for gas exchange, and the kidneys are the body's main blood filters.\n\nWould you like to learn why blood goes to the lungs before returning to the heart, instead of going directly to the body?",
      "controls": []
    }
  ],
  "actions": 10
}

