  "state": {
    "id": "state_0",
    "text": "No! 🫀 The four heart valves do NOT open at the same time. They open and close in a coordinated sequence as your heart beats.\n\nThe valves work in two pairs.\n\n1. When the ventricles relax (diastole)\nOPEN\n\nTricuspid and mitral valves\n\nCLOSED\n\nPulmonary and aortic valves\n\nBlood flows from the atria into the ventricles.\n\n2. When the ventricles contract (systole)\nOPEN\n\nPulmonary and aortic valves\n\nCLOSED\n\nTricuspid and mitral valves\n\nBlood is pumped from the ventricles into the lungs and the rest of the body.\n\nSee how the valves work\nHeart relaxes\nHeart contracts\n\nThe ventricles fill with blood while the tricuspid and mitral valves are open.\n\nEducational schematic, not an anatomical drawing. There are also brief moments when all four valves are closed.\nWhy don't all four open together?\n\nBecause blood needs to flow in one direction. If the inlet and outlet valves opened together at the wrong time, the heart couldn't build pressure and pump efficiently.\n\nOne more interesting fact: the valves don't open because the heart directly pulls them open. They open and close because of pressure differences on either side of each valve.\n\nQuick question: When the left ventricle contracts to pump blood into the aorta, should the mitral valve be open or closed?",
    "controls": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "label": "Heart relaxes",
        "disabled": false,
        "checked": true,
        "context": "Heart relaxes\nHeart contracts"
      },
      {
        "index": 1,
        "label": "Heart contracts",
        "disabled": false,
        "checked": false,
        "context": "Heart relaxes\nHeart contracts"
      }
    ]
  }
