    "text": "🫀 The Human Heart: A Beginner's Guide\n\nYour heart is one of the most important organs in your body. It works 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, even when you're sleeping!\n\nThink of your heart as a powerful pump that keeps blood moving throughout your body.\n\n1. Where is your heart located?\ncommons.wikimedia.org\n\nYour heart is:\n\nLocated in your chest, between your lungs.\n\nSlightly to the left of the center of your chest.\n\nAbout the size of your fist.\n\nProtected by your rib cage.\n\nAn adult heart typically weighs about 250–350 grams.\n\n2. The four chambers of the heart\n\nYour heart has four chambers, or rooms, that hold and move blood.\n\nkuraplan.com\n\nChamber\n\n\t\n\nJob\n\n\n\n\nRight atrium\n\n\t\n\nReceives blood returning from the body\n\n\n\n\nRight ventricle\n\n\t\n\nPumps blood to the lungs\n\n\n\n\nLeft atrium\n\n\t\n\nReceives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs\n\n\n\n\nLeft ventricle\n\n\t\n\nPumps blood to the entire body\n\nImportant: The left ventricle has the thickest muscular wall because it must pump blood throughout your body.\n\n3. How does blood flow through the heart?\n\nThe heart works with your lungs to deliver oxygen to your organs.\n\nSimplified pathway of blood circulation\n\nHere's what happens:\n\nBlood low in oxygen returns from the body to the right side of the heart.\n\nThe right side pumps blood into the lungs.\n\nThe lungs add oxygen and remove carbon dioxide.\n\nOxygen-rich blood returns to the left side of the heart.\n\nThe left side pumps it to the rest of the body.\n\nThis cycle repeats continuously.\n\n4. What makes your heart beat?\n\nYour heart has its own electrical system!\n\nA small group of specialized cells calle",
